//? Title: Midpoint circle rasterization
//? Subtitle: Actual integer decisions, reflected emissions and distinct pixel geometry
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Drawing an integer circle means choosing lattice pixels near a continuous
circular boundary. We compute one octant with integer decisions and reflect its
pixels eight ways. The report exposes actual transitions on a tiny radius,
retains the original radius12 figure, distinguishes repeated emissions from
distinct pixels, and measures radial errors without treating a finite sweep as a
universal certificate.
)

\toc

> From a midpoint test to an integer recurrence

Work in centre-relative coordinates with x initially r and y initially zero. The
next octant pixel is either east in y, (x,y+1), or southeast, (x−1,y+1). Their
separating midpoint is (x−1/2,y+1). Evaluate the circle’s implicit function
there:

$$
F=(x-1/2)^2+(y+1)^2-r^2,\qquad d=F-1/4.
$$
\alttext(The next midpoint’s implicit circle value differs from the integer decision by exactly one quarter.)

Initially d=1−r. Since x,y,r are integers, F is an integer plus1/4; F<0 exactly
when d<0. An inside midpoint selects (x,y+1); otherwise select (x−1,y+1). After
incrementing y, the east update adds2y+1; the southeast update decrements x then
adds2(y−x)+1. These follow by subtracting the two quadratic expressions, so the
producer requires additions and comparisons rather than square roots. It emits
the current pixel before updating and stops when x<y.

Eight reflections cover all octants. Axis and diagonal reflections can coincide:
the output is the original ordered emission sequence, including duplicates.
Those duplicates do not indicate extra distinct pixels. This is a boundary
rasterizer; it does not fill a disk, draw antialias coverage or connect a
contour.

> A tiny actual branch sequence

The native trace retains actual old integer states, selected branches, updated
states and the start index of their eight real reflection emissions. The diagram
reads the first three visited states of radius3.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/midpoint_circle_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t = dm::algorithms::midpoint_circle_trace(0, 0, 3);
    std::print("| actual x | actual y | actual d | actual east branch | next x | next y | next d | first " "emission |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (auto &s : t.steps)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", s.x, s.y, s.d,
                   s.east, s.x_after, s.y_after, s.d_after, s.first_point);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.steps.size(); ++i) {
        auto &s = t.steps[i];
        d.node(std::to_string(i), {6. * i, 0},
               std::format("Actual octant ({},{})\nd={}, east {}", s.x, s.y,
                           s.d, s.east));
        if (i)
            d.edge(std::to_string(i - 1), std::to_string(i));
    }
    d.save_svg("build/midpoint_circle_tiny.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| actual x | actual y | actual d | actual east branch | next x | next y | next d | first emission |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 3 | 0 | -2 | true | 3 | 1 | 1 | 0 |
| 3 | 1 | 1 | false | 2 | 2 | 2 | 8 |
| 2 | 2 | 2 | false | 1 | 3 | 7 | 16 |
\image(figures/midpoint_circle_tiny.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual radius3 integer decisions from one producer execution.)
\alttext(Each node corresponds to eight actual reflected emissions, including any symmetry duplicates.)

> The original radius12

Retain the original centre(0,0) and radius12. The native study runs the
canonical producer once, then sorts and deduplicates its actual output for
geometric summaries. The figure has equal numeric x/y limits and a square
drawable area so a circle remains circular. The error is measured with hypot on
widened coordinate differences, avoiding the legacy x*x+y*y int overflow in
diagnostics.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/midpoint_circle_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto s =
        dm::algorithms::midpoint_circle_study(0, 0,
                                              dm::algorithms::midpoint_circle_demo_radius());
    std::print("| actual quantity | value |\n| --- | ---: |\n| octant iterations | {} |\n| raw reflected " "emissions | {} |\n| distinct pixels | {} |\n| duplicate emissions | {} |\n| measured " "maximum radial error | {:.12f} |\n",
               s.analysis.iterations, s.analysis.points.size(),
               s.unique_points.size(), s.duplicate_emissions,
               s.maximum_radial_error);
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    for (auto[px, py] : s.unique_points) {
        x.push_back(px);
        y.push_back(py);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points(x, y, "distinct pixels");
    p.size(850, 850).margins(95, 35, 60, 70).x_limits(-14, 14).y_limits(-14, 14).x_label("integer x pixel centre").y_label("integer y pixel centre").title("Original radius12: distinct actual emitted pixels").save_svg("build/midpoint_circle_original.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| actual quantity | value |
| --- | ---: |
| octant iterations | 9 |
| raw reflected emissions | 72 |
| distinct pixels | 68 |
| duplicate emissions | 4 |
| measured maximum radial error | 0.369316876853 |
\image(figures/midpoint_circle_original.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Original radius12, displayed at equal coordinate scale.)
\alttext(Points are distinct actual emissions; the table separately counts the original raw reflected sequence.)

> Measured geometry and limits of the experiment

The Euclidean residual of a pixel is the absolute difference between its centre
distance and the requested radius. Raster decisions minimize a local midpoint
choice; this finite experiment reports the measured maximum for each tested
radius. It does not establish a theorem for every radius or a Hausdorff bound
between filled shapes. A translated circle has the same widened relative
coordinates and therefore the same measured residuals.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/midpoint_circle_errors.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::print("| prescribed radius | actual distinct pixels | measured max radial error |\n| ---: | ---: | " "---: |\n");
    for (int r : {1, 2, 3, 6, 12, 24, 48, 96}) {
        auto s = dm::algorithms::midpoint_circle_study(0, 0, r);
        x.push_back(r);
        y.push_back(s.maximum_radial_error);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {:.12f} |\n", r, s.unique_points.size(),
                   s.maximum_radial_error);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, y, "measured residual", {37, 99, 235});
    p.points(x, y, "", {37, 99, 235});
    p.legend({"measured residual"}, {{37, 99, 235}});
    p.x_label("prescribed integer radius").y_label("observed maximum radial error in pixels").title("Finite radius sweep: actual geometric residuals").save_svg("build/midpoint_circle_errors.svg");
}
```
